export default "## What Thyroid Disorders Do to Your Cycle\n\nThe thyroid gland controls metabolic rate throughout the body. The reproductive system is no exception. Thyroid hormones influence ovulation timing, progesterone production, and how the uterine lining builds and sheds. When thyroid function shifts, menstrual patterns follow.\n\n**Hypothyroidism** (underactive thyroid) is the more common scenario. Low thyroid hormone can delay or prevent ovulation, which disrupts the normal hormonal cascade. The result is typically heavier periods, longer bleeding, and sometimes shorter cycle lengths (periods coming more frequently). Estrogen may remain elevated relative to progesterone, causing the uterine lining to build excessively before shedding.\n\n**Hyperthyroidism** (overactive thyroid) tends to produce the opposite pattern: lighter periods, longer cycles, or missed periods. Excess thyroid hormone can suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary axis that drives ovulation.\n\nBoth conditions can also cause anovulatory cycles, where you bleed but have not actually ovulated. Anovulatory bleeding is often [irregular](/resources/condition-guides/irregular-periods-causes) in timing and volume.\n\nThis content is for informational purposes only and is not a substitute for professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment.\n\n## What to Track\n\n**Cycle length and regularity.** Track start dates and total cycle length. Hypothyroidism may shorten cycles to 21-24 days. Hyperthyroidism may stretch them past 35 days or produce skipped months. Both shifts are clinically relevant, especially if they represent a change from your baseline.\n\n**Flow volume.** Note product changes and saturation levels, especially if you notice a shift toward heavier or lighter bleeding than your usual pattern.\n\n**Energy and fatigue.** Thyroid-related fatigue has a specific quality: it is persistent, not relieved by rest, and often worst in the morning. Log energy levels daily and note whether they correlate with cycle phase or are constant regardless of where you are in your cycle. Cycle-independent fatigue points toward thyroid rather than normal [hormonal fluctuations](/resources/symptom-guides/luteal-phase-symptoms).\n\n**Temperature and cold/heat sensitivity.** Hypothyroidism causes cold intolerance. Hyperthyroidism causes heat intolerance. Note these alongside your cycle data. If you track basal body temperature, thyroid dysfunction can flatten or distort the typical post-ovulation temperature rise.\n\n**Weight changes, hair changes, and skin changes.** These are not cycle symptoms, but tracking them alongside your period data helps your provider see the full picture rather than treating menstrual complaints in isolation.\n\n## How Tracking Helps With Diagnosis and Treatment\n\nThyroid disorders are diagnosed with blood tests (TSH, free T4, sometimes free T3 and antibodies). But the decision to test often depends on the symptom picture. A person who reports heavy periods alone may get a pelvic ultrasound. A person who reports heavy periods plus fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, and thinning hair is more likely to get thyroid labs drawn.\n\nYour cycle tracking data, paired with notes on systemic symptoms, creates that clinical connection. It turns an appointment about heavy periods into a conversation about thyroid function.\n\nDuring treatment with thyroid hormone replacement, tracking your cycle provides a biological response indicator. If your periods normalize as your TSH reaches target range, that confirms the connection and the adequacy of your dose. If they do not, your provider knows to investigate additional causes.\n\n## What Your Provider Needs to See\n\nOrganize your tracking data to show:\n\n- Cycle length trend over three or more months\n- Flow volume changes compared to your baseline\n- Systemic symptoms logged consistently (fatigue severity, weight, temperature tolerance)\n- Whether cycle changes preceded, coincided with, or followed other symptoms\n- Family history of thyroid disease, if known\n\nThe timeline matters. Thyroid disorders develop gradually, and so do the menstrual changes. Showing a six-month trend is more persuasive than a single bad cycle.\n\n## Privacy Considerations for Sensitive Health Data\n\nThyroid tracking involves logging reproductive data alongside metabolic health information, medication use, and patterns related to chronic disease management. This combined health dataset should stay under your control.\n\n[Floriva](/resources/best/best-private-period-tracker-apps) keeps core cycle data, symptom logs, and health notes on your device with no account required for core tracking. Optional sync is encrypted so Floriva cannot read synced records.";
